=== QA001 | more than the eight parameters q allows
f:{[a;b;c;d;e;f;g;h;i] a}

=== clean | exactly eight is legal
f:{[a;b;c;d;e;f;g;h] a}

=== QA002 | more arguments than the literal lambda takes
{[a;b]a+b}[1;2;3]

=== clean | the right number
{[a;b]a+b}[1;2]

=== clean | an elided argument is a projection, not an error
{[a;b]a+b}[1;]

=== QA004 | applying a niladic function to the empty list
f:{[] 1};f . ()

=== clean | the same shape where the function takes an argument
f:{[a] 1};f . enlist 1

=== QA005 | each supplies one argument, so a rank-2 lambda projects
{[a;b]a+b} each 1 2 3

=== clean | each on a unary lambda applies per element
{[a]a+1} each 1 2 3

=== QA005 | a binary builtin under each projects too
cor each 1 2 3

=== clean | a unary builtin under each is the idiom
count each (1 2;3 4 5)

=== QA006 | two slots is the one arity $ has no meaning for
$[1b;2]

=== clean | three slots is the conditional
$[1b;2;3]

=== clean | five slots is a multi-way conditional
$[1b;2;0b;3;4]

=== clean | one slot is a projection, not an error
$[1b]

=== QA007 | four slots pair off with no else
r:$[a;1;b;2]

=== clean | five slots have an else
r:$[a;1;b;2;3]

=== clean | three slots is the plain conditional
r:$[a;1;2]

=== QA008 | parentheses hand a rank-2 lambda one list
f:{[a;b] a+b}
r:f(1;2)

=== QA008 | implicit y makes the rank
g:{x+y}
r:g(1;2)

=== clean | square brackets separate the arguments
f:{[a;b] a+b}
r:f[1;2]

=== clean | a rank-1 lambda takes the list as its argument
h:{x}
r:h(1;2)

=== clean | a name the file does not define is not judged
r:k(1;2)

=== QA009 | dot apply with a scalar argument
r:.[{x+y};1]

=== QA009 | the trap form is no different
r:.[f;`a;{x}]

=== clean | a list of arguments
r:.[{x+y};1 2]

=== clean | enlist makes the list
r:.[{x};enlist 1]
